*Question 1:*  Briefly state your professional goal for the semester alongside the metric you planned to use to assess your progress. 

My professional goal was to work towards building expertise in this topic via the creation and maintenance of a knowledge log. This log would contain all of the information related to the project. I would measure my success at this metric by tracking how much it grew, and how well I was able to speak about the project and topic at hand. Slip ups in communication would be tracked as well. 

*Question 2:* Please summarize your work on your goal throughout the semester. 

I did a lot to work on this goal. Early on I organized our project repo to have an organized docs directory.  It contained the general knowledge log, a glossary of terms, a directory for writeups and also a detailed sub-directory that gave links and explanations for every single source used in the project. The log itself functioned mostly as a journal for me to write down my thoughts and current understanding of the topic. I didn't update it as regularly as I would have liked, but I kept at it for a long time and by halfway into the semester this directory was an invaluable source of information for me. The last update to the "knowledge log" really was around March 8th. I stopped adding sources and entries into the log at that time. 

*Question 3:* Did you choose a meaningful goal?

I think so, yes. I constantly struggle with lacking confidence in my knowledge on topics. This helped a lot and with how many twists and turns this project took keeping all my sources organized at a minimum was amazing. Keeping all of my notes, writeups, sources and screenshots in one place gave me a very useful network of information to build from. And though I stopped updating my sources I kept referring back to them as the project progressed all the way up until the end. 

*Question 4:* How did you actively work on your goal throughout the semester?

There's not a ton to explain here really. I read a lot of stuff. I read news articles, federal highway administration guides, state level traffic analysis, anything I could find. And when I found a new source and it seemed useful I would add it to the sources folder. And I'd try at least once a week to update my thoughts on things in the knowledge log, especially during the initial exploratory phase of the project. I genuinely feel this approach was solid. I couldn't maintain it the whole semester, but it was amazing when it was going. 

